{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["(vv. 19–20) <b>You shall surely perish, etc. like the nations, etc.</b> One needs to know why He repeated saying ״אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן״, further why it was necessary to make a comparison and say <b>like the nations</b> etc., further why it was necessary to say at the end a second time <b>so shall you perish</b>, and not attach it to what He said at the beginning, ״אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן״, upon which the statement ״like the nations״ etc. revolves." "Indeed the verse may be explained according to the saying of our Sages of blessed memory (Yerushalmi Chagigah 1:7) that Jerusalem was destroyed only because they nullified the words of Torah in it, and even though they were idol worshippers, had they been studying Torah it would not have been destroyed, as they of blessed memory said. From this there would be room to sin and to worship idolatry and to take care to study Torah so that it would protect them from perishing. Therefore the statement here, when it comes to warn about the worship of idolatry that it will cause them to perish from the earth, said <b>I warn</b> etc. — meaning, I am warning you ״כִּי אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן״, and the meaning is: at first there is one loss — you shall perish — namely that you will be like the nations, lacking the Torah, so that HaShem will make them forget it and they will be like the nations; and then ״כַּגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר ה׳ מַאֲבִיד וְגוֹ׳ כֵּן תֹּאבֵדוּן״ — this is the loss of themselves. And according to this, the phrase <b>like the nations</b> is drawn upward, to give the comparison of the loss — that they will be like the nations — and it is drawn downward, to give the comparison of the loss of the nations themselves from the earth. And His saying <b>because</b> (עֵקֶב) etc. means: since there is no Torah — from this you learn that if they have Torah, even if they are idol worshippers, the Torah will stand for them. And this is what He was precise in saying <b>in the voice of</b> HaShem (בְּקוֹל ה׳), and I have already alerted you in several places that wherever ״קוֹל״ (voice) is mentioned it speaks of Torah." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend in His saying <b>like the nations</b> etc. <b>so shall you perish</b> — that besides the loss He mentioned above in the statement ״אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן״, there is yet another calamity for them: after those who remain among them perish, they will be like the nations etc. — so shall they perish — meaning that prophecy and the holy spirit will be cut off from them and they will perish. And over this the heart of the people of HaShem should tremble, for there is no curse in the world like this. Woe to the generations that tasted this bitter cup, more bitter than death. And He is merciful — may He return to have mercy upon us, that He may put His spirit in us as in days of old." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained along this line, that three kinds of punishment He alluded to here. The first — a likeness of what befell them in the destruction of the Temple, that the wicked Nebuchadnezzar killed from them whatever he killed and exiled the rest, and this is the statement <b>אָבֹד תֹּאבֵדוּן.</b> The second — a likeness of what befell them in the days of Mordecai, when the wicked Haman sought to destroy, to kill, and to annihilate all the Jews, and this is the statement <b>כַּגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר ה׳ מַאֲבִיד</b> — meaning, that He decreed upon them (Deuteronomy 20:16) ״לֹא תְחַיֶּה כָּל נְשָׁמָה״; so too shall be their punishment, and so too was Haman's decree against Israel, were it not for the merit of Mordecai and Esther who stood and nullified the decree. Therefore He repeated saying <b>כֵּן תֹּאבֵדוּן</b>, to say that this is a matter in its own right. The third — the decrees of the absence of prophecy from Israel, and this is the statement <b>because you will not hearken to the voice of</b> HaShem (עֵקֶב לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּן בְּקוֹל ה׳). The intention along this line: ״עֵקֶב״ — meaning, at the end of the reproofs and chastisements this will befall you, that you will not hear the voice of HaShem, and I have already written that there are no sufferings more bitter than the bitterness of death like this thing."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {1 "The text quotes Deuteronomy 8:20, which begins with עֵקֶב לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּן בְּקוֹל ה׳; the lemma עֵקֶב is addressed near the end of this comment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:14.811764885Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}